## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical concurrency control skills involving multiple producers, bounded buffers, and latency guarantees with a simple priority policy. The solution tests real-world challenges like starvation prevention and graceful shutdown coordination.

## Key Concepts

- Dual-queue architecture for priority separation
- Mutual exclusion and condition synchronization
- Backpressure mechanisms for flow control
- Aging algorithms to prevent starvation
- Graceful shutdown patterns with resource cleanup

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo sketch of data structures and worker loop
class Task { 
  constructor(priority) { 
    this.priority = priority; 
    this.timestamp = Date.now();
  } 
}

class PriorityQueue {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.highQueue = [];
    this.normalQueue = [];
    this.mutex = new Mutex();
    this.notEmpty = new ConditionVariable();
    this.notFull = new ConditionVariable();
    this.shutdown = false;
    this.agingThreshold = 5000; // 5 seconds
  }

  async enqueue(task) {
    await this.mutex.lock();
    while (this.size() >= this.capacity && !this.shutdown) {
      await this.notFull.wait();
    }

    if (task.priority === 'high') {
      this.highQueue.push(task);
    } else {
      this.normalQueue.push(task);
    }

    this.notEmpty.signal();
    this.mutex.unlock();
  }

  async dequeue() {
    await this.mutex.lock();
    while (this.size() === 0 && !this.shutdown) {
      await this.notEmpty.wait();
    }

    if (this.shutdown && this.size() === 0) {
      this.mutex.unlock();
      return null;
    }

    this.checkAging();

    let task;
    if (this.highQueue.length > 0) {
      task = this.highQueue.shift();
    } else if (this.normalQueue.length > 0) {
      task = this.normalQueue.shift();
    }

    this.notFull.signal();
    this.mutex.unlock();
    return task;
  }

  checkAging() {
    const now = Date.now();
    this.normalQueue = this.normalQueue.filter(task => {
      if (now - task.timestamp > this.agingThreshold) {
        this.highQueue.push(task);
        return false;
      }
      return true;
    });
  }

  size() {
    return this.highQueue.length + this.normalQueue.length;
  }

  async shutdown() {
    await this.mutex.lock();
    this.shutdown = true;
    this.notEmpty.broadcast();
    this.notFull.broadcast();
    this.mutex.unlock();
  }
}
```

## Test Plan

### Latency Testing
- Measure end-to-end processing time for high vs. normal priority tasks
- Verify high-priority tasks consistently achieve lower latency
- Test with varying load levels to validate priority guarantees

### Starvation Testing
- Generate continuous high-priority load and monitor normal task processing
- Verify aging mechanism promotes aged normal tasks
- Measure maximum wait time for normal tasks under sustained high load

### Graceful Shutdown Testing
- Initiate shutdown while tasks are processing
- Verify all in-flight tasks complete before worker termination
- Test with producers blocked on backpressure
- Confirm no resource leaks or deadlocks

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates practical reasoning about concurrency patterns—including message ordering, bounded queues, backpressure mechanisms, and fair scheduling. These are essential skills for building responsive systems without heavy infrastructure.

## Key Concepts
- Per-client FIFO event ordering to preserve client intent
- Bounded queues with backpressure to prevent memory exhaustion
- Fair round-robin scheduling to prevent client starvation
- Tick-based processing for deterministic game loop timing
- Graceful shutdown to drain pending work before exit

## Code Example
```go
type Event struct {
    ClientID uint64
    Type     string
    Payload  interface{}
}

type GameServer struct {
    clients    map[uint64]chan Event
    globalQueue chan Event
    tickRate   time.Duration
    running    atomic.Bool
}

func NewGameServer(bufferSize, globalCap int) *GameServer {
    return &GameServer{
        clients:    make(map[uint64]chan Event),
        globalQueue: make(chan Event, globalCap),
        tickRate:   16 * time.Millisecond,
    }
}

func (g *GameServer) RegisterClient(id uint64, clientBuffer int) {
    g.clients[id] = make(chan Event, clientBuffer)
}

func (g *GameServer) HandleEvent(clientID uint64, event Event) {
    select {
    case g.clients[clientID] <- event:
    default:
        old := <-g.clients[clientID]
        g.clients[clientID] <- event
    }
}

func (g *GameServer) Run(ctx context.Context) {
    g.running.Store(true)
    ticker := time.NewTicker(g.tickRate)
    defer ticker.Stop()

    for g.running.Load() {
        select {
        case <-ctx.Done():
            g.gracefulShutdown()
            return
        case <-ticker.C:
            g.processTick()
        }
    }
}

func (g *GameServer) processTick() {
    var events []Event
    for len(events) == 0 && len(g.globalQueue) > 0 {
        select {
        case e := <-g.globalQueue:
            events = append(events, e)
        default:
            break
        }
    }

    for i := 0; i < len(g.clients); i++ {
        select {
        case e := <-g.globalQueue:
            events = append(events, e)
        default:
        }
    }

    for _, e := range events {
        g.updateGameState(e)
    }
}

func (g *GameServer) gracefulShutdown() {
    for _, ch := range g.clients {
        for len(ch) > 0 {
            e := <-ch
            g.globalQueue <- e
        }
    }
    close(g.globalQueue)
}

func (g *GameServer) updateGameState(e Event) {}
```

